X('how-lcl-consolidation-works',[
 ('When LCL makes sense',['LCL, or less than container load, is for cargo that does not fill a container. As a rough guide, many shippers switch to a full container when the cargo reaches around 12 to 15 cubic metres, because the per-cubic-metre price of a full container is then lower. The exact point depends on the lane and the season, so ask for both quotes.','LCL is billed on the greater of cubic metres and weight in tonnes, so heavy and compact cargo can cost more than you expect.']),
 ('The extra handling to plan for',['Each lot is handled at the origin CFS, stuffed with other shippers\' cargo, and unstuffed and sorted at the destination. More handling means a greater chance of damage and a longer transit than a full container, usually by several days.','Because the container is shared, a delay or a customs hold on one shipper\'s cargo can affect the others.']),
 ('Tips for LCL',['Pack for handling, with strong cartons or pallets, and mark every package clearly.','Cut-offs at the CFS are earlier than for a full container, so deliver the cargo on time.','Check the destination charges. Many are charged per shipment or per cubic metre, and they can be larger than the sea freight on a small lot.'])],
 [('Is LCL slower than FCL?','Usually yes. LCL has extra handling at both ends and waits for consolidation, so it often takes several days longer than a full container.')])
X('reefer-container-guide',[
 ('Setting the temperature',['The shipper decides the set point, in degrees Celsius, based on the product. Chilled fruit, frozen meat and pharmaceuticals each have different needs, and the set point should come from the producer or the buyer in writing. Some cargo also needs a humidity or fresh air setting, and controlled-atmosphere containers are used for certain fruits on long routes.','Do not use a reefer to cool warm cargo. It is built to hold a temperature, not to bring it down.']),
 ('Typical mistakes',['Loading cargo that is not at the right temperature, which can spoil it even if the machine works properly.','Blocking the airflow with cartons placed too high or too close to the walls and doors.','Mixing products with different needs in one container, such as cargo that gives off gases that ripen or damage other goods.']),
 ('What to ask the forwarder',['Whether the container has a valid inspection and a pre-trip check.','The plug-in times at the terminal and the vessel, and what is done if the power is cut.','Whether temperature data will be recorded and shared with you, since it is your proof if there is a claim.'])],
 [('Does a reefer container cool the cargo?','It is meant to maintain a set temperature, so the cargo should be pre-cooled to that temperature before loading.')])
X('open-top-flat-rack-out-of-gauge-cargo',[
 ('How the cargo is classified',['Cargo is out of gauge, or OOG, when it exceeds the inside dimensions of a standard container, in height, width or length. It travels on a flat rack or an open top, or on break-bulk, depending on its shape and weight. Each case needs the line\'s approval, and the exact dimensions must be known at the time of booking.','The line decides the stowage, since over-height or over-width cargo can block adjacent slots, and the space is charged as if it were used.']),
 ('Planning steps',['Send the line the dimensions, weight, drawings and a photograph before the booking is confirmed.','Arrange lashing and securing material, and a surveyor if the cargo is high value or unusual.','Check the road route and the permits for moving the oversized cargo to the port, since many roads have limits.']),
 ('Cost drivers',['Slot loss: the empty slots next to the cargo that the line charges for.','Special handling at the terminal and extra lifting equipment.','The time needed for approvals, since OOG bookings often take longer to confirm than a standard box.'])],
 [('Can any container be used for tall cargo?','Only an open top or a flat rack is suitable, and over-height cargo needs the line\'s approval. The cargo must be secured properly and the dimensions declared in advance.')])
X('vgm-verified-gross-mass-guide',[
 ('The two methods',['Method one is to weigh the packed and sealed container, using a calibrated weighbridge, either at the shipper\'s site or at an approved facility. Method two is to weigh all the packages and cargo, add the weight of the pallets and packing and the tare of the container, using a certified process. The shipper chooses the method, but the second one needs approval of the process from the authority.','For many exporters, a weighbridge near the factory or the port is the simplest.']),
 ('What the declaration must include',['The container number and the verified weight in kilograms.','The method used, and the weighing facility if used.','The name and signature or electronic authorisation of the person responsible for the declaration, together with the date.']),
 ('What happens if it is wrong or late',['The line may refuse to load the container until a correct declaration is made, and the cargo can be rolled to a later vessel. Extra cost and a new cut-off follow. If the actual weight differs from the declared weight by a large margin, the line may re-weigh at the shipper\'s cost, and a wrong declaration is a safety risk because ship stability depends on it.'])],
 [('Who is responsible for the VGM?','The shipper named on the bill of lading is responsible for providing the verified gross mass to the line and the terminal before the cut-off.')])
X('container-cut-off-times-explained',[
 ('Several different cut-offs',['A booking has more than one deadline. The gate-in cut-off is the last time a container can be received at the terminal. The document cut-off is the last time for shipping instructions and customs filing. The VGM cut-off is the last time to give the weight declaration. For some cargo, such as reefers and dangerous goods, there is an earlier cut-off.','Each of these is set by the terminal or the line, and they differ between ports and vessels, so always ask for them in writing.']),
 ('Working back from the sailing',['A sample plan for an export: vessel sails on day 10. Gate-in closes on day 7, so the container must be stuffed and ready by day 6. The shipping bill is filed on day 5 and the customs examination, if any, must be done by day 6. The cargo is ready at the factory by day 4. Add a buffer day, since a missed gate-in can mean a week\'s wait for the next vessel.']),
 ('When cut-offs change',['Lines can advance the cut-off when the vessel is ahead of schedule or the port is congested. Keep in touch with your forwarder in the last days, and do not rely on the first date you were given.'])],
 [('What happens if I miss the gate-in cut-off?','The line may refuse the container for that vessel. You will need to move it to the next sailing and may pay extra storage or a rebooking charge.')])
X('container-seals-and-numbers',[
 ('How the number is built',['Every container has a four-letter owner code, a six-digit serial number and a check digit, such as ABCU 123456 7. The check digit is worked out from the other characters, so a number that is mistyped usually fails the check. The size and type code appears next to it.','The container number is the key that links the booking, the bill of lading, the customs bill and the tracking record, so a single wrong digit can cause delays.']),
 ('Seals in practice',['A bolt seal or a high-security seal is fitted by the shipper after stuffing, and the seal number is recorded on the documents. Customs may apply its own seal after an examination or for a movement under bond. The line records the number at the gate.','Keep a photograph of the seal on the closed door and of the stuffed container.']),
 ('What to check on arrival',['The seal is intact and the number matches the bill of lading.','The container number matches the documents, and the container shows no damage.','If a seal is missing or different, note it before the container is opened, and tell the line and your insurer straight away. It protects your claim.'])],
 [('Why must the seal number be on the bill of lading?','It records that the container was sealed at origin with that seal. A different or broken seal at destination is a sign of possible tampering and should be recorded before opening.')])
X('cargo-insurance-guide',[
 ('Clause A, B and C in plain words',['Clause C covers the main major casualties, such as fire, grounding, sinking and collision. Clause B adds more events, such as earthquake, lightning and water entering the vessel. Clause A is all risks, covering physical loss or damage from any external cause, except the exclusions in the policy. For most general cargo, Clause A is the standard choice, while C is a cheaper cover for bulk cargo.']),
 ('What the policy does not usually cover',['Loss caused by inadequate or unsuitable packing.','Normal leakage or loss in weight, and loss from delay.','Inherent vice, which means the nature of the goods, such as fruit that rots in transit without any outside cause.','War and strikes, which need their own clauses.']),
 ('How to insure and claim',['Insure for the CIF value plus a margin, often ten per cent, so that the profit is covered. Name the right insured party and the exact voyage.','If cargo is damaged, tell the carrier and the insurer straight away, record the damage on the delivery receipt and keep the packaging for the surveyor.','Send the claim documents promptly: bill of lading, invoice, packing list, survey report and the notice to the carrier.'])],
 [('Is the carrier\'s liability enough?','Usually not. The carrier\'s liability is limited by the contract and the law, often to a small amount per package or per kilogram, and it does not cover many causes of loss. Cargo insurance protects the full value.')])
X('general-average-explained',[
 ('A simple example',['A ship has a fire and the crew floods the hold to put it out, damaging some cargo with water. The sacrifice made to save the ship and the rest of the cargo is shared between all the parties in proportion to the value they had on the voyage. A shipper whose cargo was not touched may be asked to pay a share of the loss and the costs of saving the voyage.']),
 ('What you will be asked to do',['The shipping line declares general average and appoints an average adjuster, who works out each party\'s share. Cargo owners are usually asked to give a security, such as a guarantee, before the line will release the cargo. A cargo insurer can give this guarantee on the owner\'s behalf, if the cargo is insured.','Do not ignore the notice, because the cargo can be held until security is given.']),
 ('Why it matters',['Without insurance, a cargo owner can be asked to pay a large sum for cargo that arrived safely, or to wait for the final adjustment, which can take a long time. With cargo insurance, the insurer normally pays the contribution, which is one of the strong reasons to insure even when the cargo seems safe.'])],
 [('Do I have to pay general average if my cargo was not damaged?','You may. The sacrifice is shared between all cargo interests and the ship, and cargo may be held until you give security. A cargo policy normally covers the contribution.')])
X('cbm-calculation-guide',[
 ('A worked example',['A carton measures 60 cm by 40 cm by 50 cm. The volume is 0.6 × 0.4 × 0.5 = 0.12 cubic metres. A shipment of 80 cartons is 80 × 0.12 = 9.6 cubic metres. If each carton weighs 18 kg, the total weight is 1,440 kg, or 1.44 tonnes. For LCL sea freight, the line normally bills the greater of the cubic metres and the tonnes, so here it is 9.6.','If the same cartons are sent by air, the volumetric weight is worked out differently, with a divisor of 6,000 cubic centimetres per kilogram in the usual practice: 9,600,000 ÷ 6,000 = 1,600 kg. The airline charges the greater of the actual and volumetric weights, so 1,600 kg.']),
 ('Common mistakes',['Using the inside size of the carton and not the outside size.','Mixing centimetres and metres in the formula.','Forgetting the pallet. Add the height and the base of the pallet if the cargo is shipped on one.']),
 ('Using the number',['A cubic metre figure helps you pick the container size and compare quotes. Use the <a href="container-load-planner" style="color:#0A6CC2">container load planner</a> when you need to see how the cartons actually fit, since the CBM figure does not tell you whether the shape suits the container.'])],
 [('Is CBM the same as weight?','No. CBM is volume in cubic metres. LCL freight is charged on the greater of the volume and the weight (one cubic metre is treated as one tonne), so both numbers matter.')])
X('air-cargo-uld-and-pallets-explained',[
 ('What the airline needs to know',['A unit load device is the pallet with a net, or the container, that the airline loads on to the aircraft. The type and size of the ULD that suits your cargo depends on the aircraft: the main deck of a freighter takes tall pallets, while the lower deck of a passenger aircraft takes smaller, curved containers. A narrow-body aircraft has far less room for large ULDs than a wide-body.','Give the airline or your forwarder the dimensions and weight of each piece, so they can plan the load.']),
 ('Planning for tall or heavy cargo',['Check the height and weight limits for the aircraft type and the route, because they differ.','Cargo that is too big for the lower deck needs a freighter or a charter, which changes the price and the schedule.','Heavy pieces need the right floor load and the right securing, and a forwarder or the airline will plan the load.']),
 ('Cost effects',['Airlines may charge by ULD for consolidated loads, so full pallets built by a forwarder can bring a lower rate per kilogram. Space on the main deck of a freighter can be cheaper per kilogram than the belly of a passenger flight, but the schedules differ.'])],
 [('Can I book a whole ULD?','Yes, through the airline or a forwarder, with a rate for the whole unit. It suits larger shipments that fill the pallet or container.')])
X('perishable-cargo-by-air-and-sea',[
 ('Mode and shelf life',['The shorter the shelf life, the stronger the case for air. Fresh flowers and soft fruits usually travel by air, while apples, citrus and onions can go by reefer container, where the cost per kilogram is far lower. Compare the total cost, including the loss from shrinkage on a longer voyage, and not only the freight rate.','The same product can suit both modes, depending on variety, packaging and the buyer\'s needs.']),
 ('Handling and paperwork',['Grade, pack and pre-cool the goods before they are loaded, and keep them in the cold chain until the vehicle is ready.','Get the certificates required by the destination: phytosanitary, health or origin, and any inspection by an export agency. Check the buyer\'s requirements for labels and pallet size.','Book space with the carrier for a confirmed flight or sailing, so the cargo is not left waiting at a terminal.']),
 ('Reducing the risk',['Share the temperature and handling instructions in writing with all parties, from the packing shed to the receiving cold store. Use a temperature logger in the cargo and ask for the data after delivery. If a claim arises, a record of the cold chain is the strongest evidence.'])],
 [('Is air freight always better for perishables?','No. It is faster, but it costs more. Many products with a longer shelf life travel well in reefer containers, so compare the total cost and the quality on arrival.')])
